# Security

## Required

- Never commit `.env`, `.env.backend`, `.env.frontend`, credentials, tokens,
  or private keys.
- Never hardcode passwords, secrets, or production configuration in source.
- Never weaken a security control to ship faster.
- The frontend must never access the database directly.
- Use HTTPS in production.
- Sanitize API output and do not expose sensitive fields.

## HTTP APIs

- Validate every external input at the backend boundary with DTOs and
  `class-validator` when an endpoint accepts input.
- Restrict CORS to the documented frontend origin before exposing the API to
  browsers.
- Enable Helmet and appropriate rate limiting before exposing public routes.
- Use a Content Security Policy for frontend deployments.

## Authentication

Only implement authentication when an approved feature specification and the
relevant global product contract require it. When it is required:

- Use short-lived access tokens and secure refresh or session handling.
- Hash passwords with bcrypt at a cost of at least 10.
- Rate-limit authentication routes.
- Authorize every protected backend operation.

## Persistence

When a project adopts a database:

- Use parameterized queries.
- Give database users the least privileges required.
- Define a backup and recovery approach before production use.
- Do not expose persistence entities directly from HTTP controllers.

## Change checklist

- [ ] No secrets or sensitive values in the diff
- [ ] External input is validated when routes accept it
- [ ] Routes are authenticated and authorized when required
- [ ] Sensitive response fields and logs are excluded
